\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Chapter 1: The Storm\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 The wind came off the sea like a held breath finally let go. Mara Quinn pulled the door shut behind her and leaned on it until the latch caught.\
\'93You're late,\'94 said Tom Hale, without looking up from the lamp.\
\'93The path was underwater,\'94 Mara said. \'93I had to go round by the chapel.\'94\
\'93Then you'll be wanting tea.\'94\
\'93I'll be wanting a towel first.\'94\
\'93Towels are in the chest. Same as always.\'94\
\'93Same as always,\'94 she repeated, and almost smiled.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf0 He hasn't asked about the letter,
\f1\i0  Mara thought. 
\f2\i Maybe he doesn't know.
\f1\i0 \
Tom set down the wick trimmer. He was a broad man gone narrow with age, sixty if he was a day, and when he spoke again his voice had the gravel of a man who had shouted over gales for forty years. \'93Doctor Voss came by. Asked after you.\'94\
\'93What did you tell her?\'94\
\'93That you'd be back when the sea let you.\'94\
\uc0\u8258 \
A knock shook the door. Pip Aelwyn stood on the step, soaked to the skin, a lantern swinging from one small fist.\
\'93Miss Quinn! Miss Quinn, the boat's come loose from the jetty and Da says\'97\'94\
\'93Slow down, Pip.\'94\
\'93\'97Da says if it goes on the rocks we'll none of us eat till spring!\'94\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 Chapter 2: The Letter\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Doctor Edda Voss arrived the next morning with her bag and her clipped, careful vowels, every word set down as if it might break. She had come from Hamburg twenty years ago and never quite let the north of Germany go.\
\'93You have been avoiding me, Mara.\'94\
\'93I've been busy.\'94\
\'93Busy.\'94 The doctor folded her gloves. \'93Your father wrote to me before he died. I think you should read what he wrote.\'94\
She laid an envelope on the table. The handwriting on it was small and slanted.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 My dear girl \'97 if Edda has given you this, then I was too much of a coward to say it myself. The light was never mine to leave you. It belongs to Kerensa.\
\'93Who is Kerensa?\'94 Mara whispered.\
\'93Read the rest.\'94\
\'93I can't.\'94\
\'93You can. You are your father's daughter.\'94\
\'93That's what frightens me.\'94\
Pip had crept in behind them, dripping on the flagstones. \'93Is it treasure? Da says old Quinn had treasure.\'94\
\'93Out,\'94 said the doctor, not unkindly.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 Chapter 3: The Keeper\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 That night Tom told her the story. He told it the way he told everything, in the soft burr of the Highlands he had never lost, though he'd left Inverness as a boy of twelve.\
\'93Your father and I kept this light together for thirty years,\'94 he said. \'93And in all that time he never once lied to me. Except about Kerensa.\
\'93She was his sister. Not dead, as he told the village. Gone to sea, and never written, and him too proud to look for her.\'94\
\'93Is she alive?\'94\
\'93Aye. In Penzance. She wrote to me last spring.\'94\
\'93And you didn't tell me.\'94\
\'93I'm telling you now, lass.\'94\
}